<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qing Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qing Dynasty, Qing China, Qing dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Qing Empire was the last imperial dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>, who conquered Ming China from 1644 and ruled until 1912. It expanded China's territory to include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and presided over great population and commercial growth. Weakened in the nineteenth century by rebellions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opium-wars">Opium Wars</a> and defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>, it ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qing-empire.jpg" alt="Qing Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: zh:清朝政府 Vector: Sodacan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_China_(1889%E2%80%931912).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By 1895, Japan had sufficient industrial capacity to defeat <b>Qing China</b>; by 1905, to sustain a major war against Russia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…of Punakha (1910) then made Bhutan a de facto protectorate of the British Empire, whose protection guarded it from Tibet and <b>Qing China</b>; the friendship treaty with India of 1949 took over that role, and the border with China was closed in 1960 after an influx of refugees.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bhutan">Kingdom of Bhutan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the same time Burma faced four invasions by the <b>Qing Empire</b> between 1765 and 1769; the Burmese defeated them all, destroying the army of Ming Rui at Maymyo in 1768 and surrounding the last Qing army at Kaungton, which led to a negotiated peace in 1769.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Liao was the first of the so-called conquest dynasties, and its combination of steppe and Chinese government influenced the Jin dynasty of the Jurchen, the Mongol Yuan and, through them, the <b>Manchu Qing</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In April 1644 Li Zicheng's rebels captured Beijing and the Chongzhen Emperor took his own life; the Ming general Wu Sangui then allied with the Manchus, who defeated Li at Shanhai Pass and occupied Beijing, establishing the <b>Qing dynasty</b> there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nguyen built one of the most thoroughly Confucian states in Vietnamese history, consciously modelled on <b>Qing China</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
